Specifications only for level 6 (behaviour and wire facts; no code, comment or table copied), the same Ubuntu kernel and commit,
read for what the chip and the firmware expect and in which order; every fact landed in WifiHw.h, and the writers, parsers and
the step machine on top of them (WifiCe.h, WifiHtc.h, WifiWmi.h, WifiLink.h) are this repository's own:
  ath12k/htc.c and htc.h: the transport's 8-byte header and its fields, the four control messages (ready, connect a service,
    its answer, set-up complete) with each word's layout, the connection flags (the receive allocation, "no credit flow
    control"), the connect statuses, the trailer's records and the credit report's shape, that only a message interface keeps
    credit flow control, and that a message costs ceil(length / credit size) credits.
  ath12k/wmi.c and wmi.h: the command and event identifiers level 6 uses, the 4-byte command header, the TLV form (length in
    the low half, tag in the high half, the value padded to a word) and the four array tags; the field order of the
    service-ready, ready, scan and management-frame events; the initialisation command's three TLVs and the field order of the
    resource configuration's 72 words with the two flag words its host always sends; the start-scan structure's 39 words, the
    scan flags and the dwell times its host uses, the five TLVs that follow it and that the address array carries the broadcast
    address; the stop-scan and virtual-device commands; the scan event types and reasons.
  ath12k/wifi7/wmi.c and hw.h: the resource-configuration values of this chip's own entry (four virtual devices, sixteen peers,
    thirty-two traffic identifiers, the receive timeouts, native-Wi-Fi decapsulation, the message-descriptor count, peer-map
    version 1, "use a radio identifier") and the target constants behind them.
  ath12k/ce.c, ce.h, hal.c, wifi7/hal.c, wifi7/hal.h, wifi7/hal_wcn7850.c, wifi7/hal_qcc2072.c, wifi7/hal_desc.h: that a pipe is
    a source ring, a destination ring and a destination-status ring; that both the source and the destination ring are producer
    rings of the host's while the status ring is the chip's; the three descriptor layouts and their fields (the address's high
    bits, the length, the identifier that comes back in the status entry, the ring identifier); the register block of a ring
    (base low and high with the size in words, the identifier with the descriptor's size, the pointer-address pair, the
    interrupt set-up, the enable bit in the misc register), the R2 block at +0x400 with the host's pointer, the
    destination-status ring's own block at +0x58 and its pointer at +0x408, the largest-buffer register of a destination ring,
    the units at 0x01b80000 and 0x2000 apart, the ring identifiers (64, 81 and 100 plus the pipe), **that both pointers count
    32-bit words**, and that the chip writes its own pointer into a host-memory block the ring names.
  ath12k/wifi7/pci.c and wifi7/hw.c: that 0x110e is this chip, its hardware entry (the board size, the auxiliary microcode, the
    internal sleep clock, nine copy engines, fourteen service entries, shadow registers supported) and the window register
    0x3278 it shares with the QCC2072.
  ath12k/core.c: the order the whole start-up runs in (the transport up, its ready awaited, the services connected, set-up
    complete, the service-ready event, the initialisation, the ready event) and that the host memory chunks are always none.
  ath12k/mac.c: what a scan needs (a created virtual device, which this chip does not have to start), the scan identifier, and
    that a passive scan asks for no network by name.
